@page "/beyond"
@inject IBeyondClaudeService BeyondClaude
@inject IMeshTransportService Mesh

@*
    Beyond Code: what Concierge is for once it stops being a coding tool.

    This was a marketing page, and the comment that used to sit here said it
    had stopped being one. It had not. Eleven capabilities were listed under
    "What it can do", each with a green Ready beside it, and six of them did not
    exist in any form — no memory store, no helper teams, no simulations, no
    server access, no small-machine profiles, no security checks. Reading the
    room, you would believe the product did all eleven today.

    Two lists now. What is here names the thing in the app that delivers it, so
    anybody can go and check. What is not built has no badge at all, because a
    direction wearing a status is how the first version of this page happened.
*@

@{
    var snapshot = BeyondClaude.GetSnapshot();
    var mesh = Mesh.GetSnapshot();
}

<div class="room">
    <div class="room-top">
        <a class="room-back" href="/">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="15 6 9 12 15 18"/></svg>
            Workspace
        </a>
        <h1>Beyond Code</h1>
    </div>

    <p class="room-lede">
        Concierge started as something that writes code. These are the directions it
        goes in beyond that — what is here today, what is not yet, and the limit each
        one works inside.
    </p>

    <div class="room-body">
        @{
            var here = snapshot.Capabilities.Where(c => c.State != BeyondState.NotBuilt).ToList();
            var ahead = snapshot.Capabilities.Where(c => c.State == BeyondState.NotBuilt).ToList();
        }

        <RoomSection Title="Here now" Count="@here.Count" Open="true">
            <div class="room-grid">
                @foreach (var capability in here)
                {
                    <article class="room-card">
                        <div class="room-card-top">
                            <span class="room-card-name">@capability.PlainEnglishName</span>
                            <span class="state">
                                <span class="dot @(capability.State == BeyondState.InTheApp ? "dot-done" : "dot-waiting")" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                @(capability.State == BeyondState.InTheApp ? "In the app" : "Needs a key")
                            </span>
                        </div>
                        <p>@capability.Summary</p>
                        @* Where to go and look. A claim nobody can check is the
                           thing this room was made of. *@
                        <p class="room-note">Where: @capability.Where</p>
                        <p class="room-note">Limit: @capability.SafetyBoundary</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </RoomSection>

        @* Three, then the rest on request. Eleven cards ran the room 495px past
           the bottom of the window. *@
        <RoomSection Title="Not built yet" Count="@ahead.Count">
            <p class="room-note">
                Directions, not features. Nothing below is in the app, and none of it is
                scheduled — it is written down so the list is honest about its own size
                rather than quietly shorter than the ambition.
            </p>
            <div class="room-grid">
                @foreach (var capability in (_allCapabilities ? ahead : ahead.Take(3)))
                {
                    <article class="room-card">
                        <div class="room-card-top">
                            <span class="room-card-name">@capability.PlainEnglishName</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>@capability.Summary</p>
                        <p class="room-note">Limit it would work inside: @capability.SafetyBoundary</p>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
            @if (!_allCapabilities && ahead.Count > 3)
            {
                <button class="ws-more" type="button" @onclick="@(() => _allCapabilities = true)">
                    Show all @ahead.Count
                </button>
            }
        </RoomSection>

        @* Titled "Where it remembers things", with "Follows you" beside every row —
           a statement about storage, for a store that does not exist. There is no
           memory service, no file, nothing written and nothing read; the four below
           are a shape somebody sketched. "Follows you" in particular is a promise
           about a device somebody else is holding.

           The rows stay, because the shape is worth keeping. The tense does not. *@
        <RoomSection Title="What it would remember" Count="@snapshot.MemoryRooms.Count">
            <p class="room-note">
                Part of "Remember useful work", which is not built. Nothing is stored in
                these yet — they are what the rooms would be for.
            </p>
            <div class="rows">
                @foreach (var room in snapshot.MemoryRooms)
                {
                    <div class="row">
                        <span class="row-name">
                            @room.Name
                            <span class="row-sub">@room.Purpose</span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="row-val">@(room.PortableAcrossDevices ? "Would follow you" : "Would stay on this device")</span>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </RoomSection>

        <RoomSection Title="Mesh">
            <div class="rows">
                <div class="row">
                    <span class="row-name">This node</span>
                    <span class="row-val">@mesh.NodeTag</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row">
                    <span class="row-name">Peers it knows</span>
                    <span class="row-val">@mesh.KnownPeers</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row">
                    <span class="row-name">Waiting to send</span>
                    <span class="row-val">@mesh.PendingBundles</span>
                </div>
                <div class="row">
                    <span class="row-name">Transport</span>
                    <span class="state">
                        <span class="dot @(mesh.IsActive ? "dot-done" : "dot-waiting")" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        @(mesh.IsActive ? "Running" : "Not running")
                    </span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="room-note">@mesh.Summary</p>
        </RoomSection>

        <RoomSection Title="Picking work back up">
            <p class="room-note">@snapshot.SafeResume.Summary</p>
            <div class="rows">
                <div class="row"><span class="row-name">Asks before resuming</span><span class="row-val">@YesNo(snapshot.SafeResume.RequiresUserApproval)</span></div>
                <div class="row"><span class="row-name">Keeps the original sandbox</span><span class="row-val">@YesNo(snapshot.SafeResume.KeepsOriginalSandbox)</span></div>
                <div class="row"><span class="row-name">Refuses to widen permissions</span><span class="row-val">@YesNo(snapshot.SafeResume.BlocksPermissionBypass)</span></div>
                <div class="row"><span class="row-name">Stops when the context changes</span><span class="row-val">@YesNo(snapshot.SafeResume.StopsWhenContextChanges)</span></div>
            </div>
        </RoomSection>
    </div>
</div>
